{"id":"source-map.json","pointer":"/source_steps","view":"value","value":[{"id":"S001","text":"Fix x∈V and r>0. H002 supplies continuous derivatives of every order near (x,r). H004 implies u·u=1. For the time-curve applications, extend u and its positive-time derivatives by zero at nonpositive times; these extensions agree with the original functions near every r>0. Apply M001 to the square of each real component of this total time curve and add the m derivatives. Differentiating this constant identity gives 2sum_{k=1}^m u_k (u_t)_k=2u·u_t=0, hence u·u_t=0. Since the time and point were arbitrary this identity holds on the entire open domain."},{"id":"S002","text":"Choose the pointwise scalar a using H005. Dot u_tt−Δu=a u with u_t. By F001 and symmetry of the real dot product, u_t·(u_tt−Δu)=a(u_t·u)=0. This uses only the value of a at the chosen point, with no differentiation or regularity of a."},{"id":"S003","text":"Differentiate the identity in F001 in time, using M001 on each component product and H002 to supply both derivatives: |u_t|^2+u·u_tt=0. For a spatial index j, differentiating u·u=1 in x_j by M001 first gives u·u_j=0 at every point. Differentiate that identity once more in x_j, again by M001, to obtain |u_j|^2+u·u_jj=0. These finite component sums establish u·u_tt=−|u_t|^2 and u·u_jj=−|u_j|^2 for every j."},{"id":"S004","text":"Dot the chosen normality equation with u. H004 gives |u|^2=1; F003 and Δu=sum_j u_jj therefore give a=u·(u_tt−Δu)=sum_j|u_j|^2−|u_t|^2. The same calculation applies to any real scalar satisfying that equation, hence gives the claimed universally conditional scalar formula. Substituting it back gives the explicit wave-map equation. F001, F002 and F003 give all remaining clauses. Generalize over x and r; the existentially selected scalar does not escape."},{"id":"S005","text":"Fix t0>0 and put J=[t0/2,3t0/2]. Its endpoints satisfy 0<t0/2<3t0/2, so H006 supplies a compact spatial set C on whose complement u=q for every time in J. Compact sets in Euclidean space are closed; hence (V\C)×int J is open. At each of its points u agrees with the constant q on a neighborhood. Every positive-order derivative is therefore zero there: difference quotients of a locally constant function are zero, and this applies successively to all orders. H002 gives continuity of all derivatives on the entire open domain."},{"id":"S006","text":"Apply M009 to the compact set C and the identity map V→V. The identity is continuous, so it gives a real uniform norm bound even when C is empty."},{"id":"S007","text":"Choose L with the bound of F001 and set R=max(L,0)+1>0 and Q=[−R,R]^n. For x∈C, every coordinate satisfies |x_j|≤|x|≤L<R, so C⊂(−R,R)^n=int Q. H001 gives positive finite dimensions; Q is a compact measurable coordinate box. M010 with all lower endpoints −R and upper endpoints R gives |Q|=∏_{j=1}^n(2R)=(2R)^n<∞."},{"id":"S008","text":"Fix r∈int J. Smoothness in H002 and the finite component formulas defining e,A,B_j,T_j,g_j imply continuity of these functions and of e_t and ∂_{x_j}g_j. The zero derivatives proved in S001 make all of these sections zero outside C. Their nonzero sets have closures contained in the closed compact set C, so each has compact support. Apply M003 to each section with X=V, Z=R and μ=dx: Euclidean Borel open sets are measurable, and Lebesgue measure is finite on compact sets. Thus every listed section is integrable. Apply M009 on Q to each continuous scalar section to obtain a finite absolute bound on Q; together with zero values off Q, the usual absolute integral bound is that constant times |Q|. This also proves the supplied bounded-support estimate."},{"id":"S009","text":"Since V\C is open and contains ∂Q, every section in F003 vanishes on a neighborhood of ∂Q. The time t0 belongs to int J, so e(·,t0) is integrable. Generalize over the arbitrary t0. The same argument supplies the complete existential common-support assertion and all the asserted bounds for each t0; in particular it proves integrability at every positive time."},{"id":"S010","text":"Fix x∈V, r>0 and a spatial index j. By H002 every derivative entering u_t,u_tt,u_j,u_jj,u_jt is continuous and has derivatives of every order. Their finite sums and component products, in particular e and g_j, are jointly smooth by the usual finite differentiation rules."},{"id":"S011","text":"Define U(y,ρ)=u(y,ρ) for ρ>0 and zero for ρ≤0. Near (x,r) it agrees with u since r>0. H002 gives that U is C^2 at this point. Apply M002 over R to U at (x,r), with smoothness order 2; the real minimum smoothness is 2. Evaluate the symmetric second derivative on the time direction (0,1) and the jth spatial coordinate direction. Coordinate directional derivatives are the corresponding partial derivatives, so ∂_r u_j(x,r)=∂_{x_j}u_t(x,r)=u_jt(x,r). This works at every positive time and point."},{"id":"S012","text":"Extend each time curve below by zero at nonpositive times; this does not change it near the chosen r>0. Apply M001 over R to each squared component of u_t in the time variable and to each squared component of u_j in the time variable. Add the finitely many component derivatives and use F002 to replace ∂_r u_j by u_jt. This yields e_t=2u_t·u_tt+2sum_{i=1}^n u_i·u_it=2A+2sum_{i=1}^n B_i. Apply M001 to each component product in g_j along the x_j-coordinate line: ∂_{x_j}g_j=u_jt·u_j+u_t·u_jj=B_j+T_j. F001 gives continuity of all terms. Generalize over x,r,j to obtain all the asserted identities and regularity."},{"id":"S013","text":"Fix t0>0. Choose C,R from N002 and use its J=[t0/2,3t0/2], Q=[−R,R]^n. Put η=t0/4>0 and I=[t0−η,t0+η]⊂int J. N003 makes e_t continuous on Q×I. This product is compact, since Q and the closed bounded interval I are compact."},{"id":"S014","text":"Apply M009 to the continuous scalar function e_t on the compact product Q×I. It gives a real finite bound L for |e_t| on this product."},{"id":"S015","text":"Choose L from F001 and put K=max(L,0)≥0. For 0<|h|<η fix any y∈V. First consider y∈Q and apply M005 to ρ↦e(y,ρ), extended by zero when ρ≤0, on [t0,t0+h] if h>0, and on [t0+h,t0] if h<0. These nondegenerate intervals lie in I, the function is smooth there by N003, and its derivative is e_t with absolute value at most K by F001. The within-interval derivative agrees with the ordinary derivative on these intervals. The resulting estimate is |e(y,t0+h)−e(y,t0)|≤K|h|, hence the absolute quotient is at most K. For y∉Q, both densities are zero by N002, since both times belong to int J. Therefore the quotient is bounded on V by K 1_Q."},{"id":"S016","text":"Define Φ(ζ,y) to be this quotient when 0<|ζ|<η and zero otherwise, and define β=K1_Q. For those small nonzero ζ each quotient is continuous in y, hence strongly measurable, and is integrable as a linear combination of the two integrable density sections supplied by N002. N002 gives measurable Q with finite measure. The nonnegative simple function β has integral K|Q|<∞, so it is integrable. N003 gives pointwise convergence Φ(ζ,y)→e_t(y,t0) as ζ→0 through nonzero values, by the definition of the derivative of e(y,·). F002 is the required domination."},{"id":"S017","text":"Apply M004 with X=V, μ=dx, Z=R, index set R and the punctured neighborhood filter at zero. This filter is countably generated: the sets {ζ:0<|ζ|<1/k}, k=1,2,..., are a countable neighborhood basis. F003 supplies measurability, domination, integrability of the bound and pointwise, therefore almost-everywhere, convergence. Thus ∫_V Φ(ζ,y)dy tends to ∫_V e_t(y,t0)dy."},{"id":"S018","text":"For 0<|ζ|<η the two density sections are integrable by N002. M013, applied to e(·,t0+ζ) and −e(·,t0), and M014 with scalars −1 and 1/ζ give ∫_V Φ(ζ,y)dy=(E(t0+ζ)−E(t0))/ζ. Here E is legitimate because N002 proved integrability at every positive time, and t0+ζ>0. Substituting into F004 proves differentiability of the actual energy and E'(t0)=∫_V e_t(y,t0)dy. Generalize over t0>0."},{"id":"S019","text":"Fix t0>0 and 1≤j≤n. Choose C,R from N002 and put Q=[−R,R]^n. At t0, g_j, its jth derivative and the two summands B_j,T_j are continuous, integrable, and zero outside C⊂int Q. N003 gives smoothness and the pointwise identity ∂_{x_j}g_j=B_j+T_j."},{"id":"S020","text":"Define Xhat to be the tuple space of all coordinates except j with product Lebesgue measure, and χ(x)=((x_i)_{i≠j},x_j). Apply M011 with predicate i≠j to split the finite product measure. The remaining block {j} has a unique element; M017 identifies its product measure with ordinary one-dimensional Lebesgue measure by evaluation at j. Composing these explicit measurable equivalences shows that χ is a measure-preserving measurable equivalence from V to Xhat×R. The inverse is coordinate insertion. When n=1, Xhat is the one-point empty product with mass one, so the same identification applies."},{"id":"S021","text":"Fix any y∈Xhat and define G(z)=g_j(χ^{-1}(y,z),t0). By N003 this function is smooth and G'(z)=∂_{x_j}g_j(χ^{-1}(y,z),t0). If z=±R, the inserted point lies outside C, since C⊂(−R,R)^n; hence G(−R)=G(R)=0. The derivative is continuous on the finite interval [−R,R], so it is interval integrable there. M006 gives ∫_{−R}^R G'(z)dz=G(R)−G(−R)=0. This is also the integral on the closed interval: its endpoints have Lebesgue measure zero. For |z|>R the derivative vanishes by F001; thus ∫_{z∈ℝ} ∂_{x_j}g_j(χ^{-1}(y,z),t0) dz=0. This proves the zero inner integral for every y, including when some other coordinate lies outside the box."},{"id":"S022","text":"Define H(y,z)=∂_{x_j}g_j(χ^{-1}(y,z),t0). It is continuous by N003 and the explicit coordinate insertion map, and vanishes outside the compact product box corresponding to Q by F001. Thus its support closure is compact. Apply M003 on Xhat×R with its product Lebesgue measure to obtain integrability. Apply M016 with the coordinate equivalence χ and scalar function H: ∫_V ∂_{x_j}g_j(x,t0)dx=∫_{Xhat×R}H. Apply M007 to this integrable H. The product Lebesgue factors are sigma-finite, hence s-finite, including the empty factor. F003 makes each inner integral zero. Therefore ∫_V ∂_{x_j}g_j(x,t0)dx=∫_{Xhat}0=0. Restricting to Q gives the identical box integral, since its integrand vanishes off Q. This is precisely the coordinate FTC/Fubini integration-by-parts step."},{"id":"S023","text":"Integrate the identity in F001 and use M013 with its two integrable summands B_j(·,t0),T_j(·,t0). F004 yields 0=∫_V B_j+∫_V T_j. Rearranging gives ∫_V B_j=−∫_V T_j. Unfold the definitions of B and T to get exactly the asserted vector-dot-product formula. Generalize over t0>0 and all spatial indices j."},{"id":"S024","text":"Fix t0>0. N002 supplies integrability of e and every derivative product at this time. N004 gives E'(t0)=∫_V e_t(x,t0)dx. N003 gives e_t=2A+2sum_{j=1}^n B_j pointwise."},{"id":"S025","text":"Use M013 for the two terms in e_t, M014 to take the factors 2 outside the integrals, and M012 for the finite sum of integrable B_j sections. Then use N005 for every j. This retains the exact calculation E'(t0)=2∫_V u_t·u_tt dx+2sum_j∫_V u_j·u_jt dx=2∫_V u_t·u_tt dx−2sum_j∫_V u_t·u_jj dx. Every integral is finite by F001."},{"id":"S026","text":"Apply M012 also to the finite family T_j and use M013 and M014 with scalar −1 to combine the difference of integrals. By finite distributivity of the dot product and Δu=sum_j u_jj, A−sum_j T_j=u_t·(u_tt−Δu) pointwise. Thus F002 becomes E'(t0)=2∫_V u_t(x,t0)·(u_tt(x,t0)−Δu(x,t0))dx. N001 makes this integrand zero at every x, so its integral and E'(t0) are zero."},{"id":"S027","text":"The time t0 was arbitrary. N002 gives integrability for every positive time, N004 gives differentiability on (0,∞), and F003 gives the zero derivative there. The definitions e=|u_t|^2+sum_j|u_j|^2 and E=∫e preserve the required energy normalization without a factor 1/2."},{"id":"S028","text":"For completeness fix arbitrary s,t>0. If s<t, restrict E to [s,t]; F004 gives differentiability there and within derivative equal to its ordinary derivative zero. M005 with bound 0 gives |E(t)−E(s)|≤0(t−s)=0, hence E(t)=E(s). If t<s, apply the same argument on [t,s], and if s=t equality is immediate. The source expects a total real function: extend E arbitrarily outside (0,∞); it agrees with E on an open neighborhood of either closed interval, so its derivatives there are unchanged. Generalizing over s,t gives constancy. Together with F004 this proves every clause of P. All arguments concern positive times only."}],"has_more":false,"next_offset":null}
